laplace-condition-e9757ba9.interpretation:
In the equation 
\nabla \cdot \mathbf{D} = \rho_{free}
, the left side, \nabla \cdot \mathbf{D}\ , represents the divergence of the electric displacement field \mathbf{D}\ . Divergence is a vector calculus operator that measures the extent to which a vector field flows outward from a point. A positive divergence indicates that the field is expanding or originating from that point, while a negative divergence indicates that the field is contracting or terminating at that point. The right side of the equation, \rho_{free}\ , is the free electric charge density. Free charges are those that are not bound to atoms or molecules and are capable of moving freely, such as charges in conductors. Therefore, the equation fundamentally states that the sources (or sinks) of the electric displacement field are free electric charges. In regions of space containing no free charge (i.e., \rho_{free} = 0\ ), the electric displacement field is solenoidal, meaning its divergence is zero. This implies that the electric displacement field lines do not begin or end in such regions; they must either form closed loops or extend to infinity (though in electrostatics, field lines typically originate from positive charges and terminate on negative charges, or extend to infinity if charges are unbalanced).

